<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Krakow</h1><p class="st-meta">1138 CE – 1320 CE · capital Krakow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior duchy of fragmented Poland, whose throne at Krakow carried overlordship of the other Piast dukes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In his testament of 1138 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Boleslaw III Wrymouth</a> divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> among his sons, creating a senior province around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> to be held by the eldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> with authority over the others. The first senior duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile">Wladyslaw II the Exile</a>, tried to rule alone and was driven out by his brothers in 1146.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> passed in turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iv-the-curly">Boleslaw IV</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iii-the-old">Mieszko III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just">Casimir II the Just</a>, and the seniority principle broke down as rival dukes fought for the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland's</a> nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gained great influence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> devastated the duchy in 1241, but under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Boleslaw V the Chaste</a> Krakow received town rights in 1257 and the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wieliczka">Wieliczka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bochnia">Bochnia</a> brought wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Silesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus">Henry IV Probus</a> and the Bohemian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II</a>, who ruled from 1291, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> in 1306. He was crowned there in 1320, and the duchy became the core of the reunited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1025-1385).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-krakow.jpg" alt="Duchy of Krakow" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Zuber &amp; MariuszR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polska_za_W_Lokietka_(1275-1300).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1138 CE – 1320 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Krakow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wladyslaw II the Exile, Casimir II the Just, Boleslaw V the Chaste</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seniorate Province, Duchy of Lesser Poland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1229&amp;focus=duchy-of-krakow" aria-label="Duchy of Krakow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 436 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Krakow lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 692L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Krakow. <a href="/atlas?year=1229&amp;focus=duchy-of-krakow">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
